### Mastering Chip Select in Code
In SPI programming, controlling which peripheral listens is as simple as toggling a standard digital GPIO pin.

Here is a complete Arduino sketch showing how an engineer coordinates two SPI peripherals using manual Chip Select toggling:

```cpp
#include <SPI.h>

const int CS_DISPLAY = 10; // Dedicated Chip Select for LED Matrix
const int CS_SENSOR  = 9;  // Dedicated Chip Select for RFID/Sensor

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  while (!Serial);

  // CRITICAL: Configure CS pins as OUTPUT and set HIGH before SPI.begin()!
  pinMode(CS_DISPLAY, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(CS_SENSOR, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(CS_DISPLAY, HIGH); // De-select Display (Hi-Z state)
  digitalWrite(CS_SENSOR, HIGH);  // De-select Sensor (Hi-Z state)

  SPI.begin(); // Initializes hardware SPI on Pins 11, 12, 13
  Serial.println("SPI Bus Initialized with 2 CS lines.");
}

void loop() {
  // 1. TALK TO DISPLAY
  digitalWrite(CS_DISPLAY, LOW);  // Wake up Display!
  byte displayReply = SPI.transfer(0x55); // Send test byte 0x55
  digitalWrite(CS_DISPLAY, HIGH); // Put Display back to sleep

  delayMicroseconds(5);

  // 2. TALK TO SENSOR
  digitalWrite(CS_SENSOR, LOW);   // Wake up Sensor!
  byte sensorReply = SPI.transfer(0xAA);  // Send command byte 0xAA
  digitalWrite(CS_SENSOR, HIGH);  // Put Sensor back to sleep

  Serial.print("Display Reply: 0x");
  Serial.print(displayReply, HEX);
  Serial.print(" | Sensor Reply: 0x");
  Serial.println(sensorReply, HEX);

  delay(1000);
}
```

### Code Breakdown:
- `digitalWrite(CS_..., HIGH)`: Setting CS HIGH before `SPI.begin()` guarantees that neither peripheral attempts to drive MISO during system boot.
- `digitalWrite(CS_DISPLAY, LOW)`: Wakes up only the display. The sensor remains completely inactive in Hi-Z mode.
- `SPI.transfer(value)`: Clocks 8 bits out on MOSI while simultaneously reading 8 bits on MISO.
